  "articleBody": "Installing custom trim and molding can really elevate a room, but a few common blunders can turn a dream project into a DIY nightmare. Here are some mistakes to watch out for to ensure your trim looks truly professional. First off, poor miter cuts are a huge giveaway. If your corners don't meet precisely, you'll end up with unsightly gaps. Take your time, measure twice (or three times!), and use a good saw. Another big one is failing to check for level, plumb, and square as you go. Walls and floors are rarely perfect, and if you don't adjust your trim to account for these imperfections, your finished work will look crooked. Don't forget to acclimate your wood trim to your home's environment for a few days before installation; otherwise, it might expand or contract, leading to gapping after it’s installed. Leaving visible gaps between the trim and the wall is another common pitfall. This is where good caulking and wood filler come in handy for a seamless blend. Finally, skipping the prep work of filling nail holes and sanding before painting or staining can ruin the final look. For intricate profiles, many corners, or when you have high expectations for a truly seamless, professional finish, calling in a seasoned professional is always a smart move. They’ve got the experience to handle all those tiny details that make a huge difference.",
